{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib formalization of results on finite simple graphs G whose core (the vertices of maximum degree Δ) has maximum degree at most two, stated as: every vertex of degree Δ has at most two neighbours of degree Δ. Hilton–Zhao (1992): if G is connected and not Δ-edge-colourable, then G − e is Δ-edge-colourable for every edge e, every vertex has at least two neighbours of degree Δ (so the core is 2-regular), and every vertex has degree at least Δ − 1, with equality somewhere when Δ ≥ 3 (hiltonZhao_critical, hiltonZhao_two_le, hiltonZhao_core_regular, hiltonZhao_min_degree, hiltonZhao_min_degree_eq); corollaries: class one when some vertex of degree Δ has at most one neighbour of degree Δ, or some vertex has degree at most Δ − 2. For Δ = 3: a connected class-two graph is a petal graph (Cariolaro–Cariolaro Lemma 3: degrees 2 or 3, the degree-3 vertices induce a 2-regular graph, degree-2 vertices have only degree-3 neighbours) and 3|E| = 4|V|; and if some component of the core carries no internal petal (no vertex of degree below 3 adjacent to two of its vertices) then G is 3-edge-colourable (Cariolaro–Cariolaro Lemma 7, for a single core cycle). The graph P* (Petersen minus a vertex) is defined on Fin 9, proved isomorphic to the Petersen graph minus a vertex, and shown to satisfy the hypotheses, to be connected, of class two and not overfull. Cariolaro–Cariolaro (2003): a connected graph with Δ = 3 whose core has maximum degree at most two is not 3-edge-colourable if and only if it is isomorphic to P* (cariolaro_iff, cariolaro). The proof recolours one core cycle with its petals in a colouring of G − e (criticality): a combinatorial chorded-cycle lemma (a cyclic sequence of vertices with chords and external restrictions has a proper 3-edge-colouring whose missing colours differ along chords and avoid the restrictions, unless it is the hexagon with three antipodal chords or a chordless odd cycle with constant restrictions) proved by induction with safe local reductions; the arc reduction rests on a finite automaton whose reachable states are checked by decide. Vizing's theorem and Vizing's adjacency lemma are lake dependencies on PALOMAR-2026-10-15-000004 and PALOMAR-2026-11-04-000009; Kempe chains and their end-point lemma come from the former.",
